@*
    Type anything, or choose from a list that always opens.

    The browser's own datalist cannot do the second part: it filters itself by whatever is in the
    box, so the moment a name is entered the list shrinks to that one entry and appears to stop
    working. There is no way to turn that off, hence this.
*@

@*
    Closing is left to the stylesheet, which hides the list whenever focus is not inside the box.
    Closing it on the input losing focus, as this did, meant tabbing towards the options dismissed
    them on the way — so the list was reachable with a mouse and with nothing else. The toggle is
    tabbable now for the same reason: it was the only way to open the list, and it could not be
    reached.

    The toggle also takes focus when it is pressed, where it used to refuse it to keep the input's.
    That refusal is what the stylesheet cannot live with: pressed with nothing else in the box
    focused, it would open a list that focus was not inside, and the rule below would hide it again
    at once. The options still refuse focus, so choosing one leaves it on the toggle — inside the
    box either way.
*@
<div class="sem-combo @(_open ? "open" : null)" @onkeydown="Dismiss">
    <input type="text"
           id="@Id"
           value="@Value"
           disabled="@Disabled"
           placeholder="@Placeholder"
           @onchange="e => Commit(e.Value?.ToString())"
           @onfocus="() => _open = false" />

    <button type="button"
            class="toggle"
            disabled="@Disabled"
            title="@(_open ? "Close the list" : "Show every option")"
            aria-label="@(_open ? "Close the list" : "Show every option")"
            aria-expanded="@(_open ? "true" : "false")"
            @onclick="() => _open = !_open">▾</button>

    @if (_open && Options.Count > 0)
    {
        <ul class="options">
            @foreach (var option in Options)
            {
                @*
                    Suppressing the mousedown keeps the input from losing focus before the click
                    lands, which is what would otherwise close the list out from under the pointer.
                *@
                <li>
                    <button type="button"
                            class="@(option == Value ? "selected" : null)"
                            @onmousedown:preventDefault="true"
                            @onclick="() => Commit(option)">@option</button>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }
</div>

@code {
    private bool _open;

    /// <summary>Escape closes the list, wherever inside the box the keyboard happens to be.</summary>
    private void Dismiss(KeyboardEventArgs e)
    {
        if (e.Key == "Escape")
        {
            _open = false;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>The identifier a label points at.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Id { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the box currently holds.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Everything the list offers, shown in full however much has been typed.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required IReadOnlyList<string> Options { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Text shown while the box is empty.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Placeholder { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the field is offered at all.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Shown and disabled rather than hidden, where a setting makes it meaningless: an heir's title
    /// is nothing to an elected government, and a box that vanishes leaves a reader wondering what
    /// used to be there. Whatever a design already holds is kept and still written back.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Called with whatever was typed or chosen.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnChange { get; set; }

    private async Task Commit(string? value)
    {
        _open = false;
        await OnChange.InvokeAsync(value ?? string.Empty);
    }
}
